# alb device agent config.
# Copy this file to agent.conf (same folder), fill in hub_url + token,
# then double-click run-agent.bat.
#
# Format: one KEY=VALUE per line. No quotes needed. Comments only on their
# own line — a '#' inside a value is kept as-is. Command-line flags override
# anything set here.

# The hub's signaling endpoint. ws:// on a trusted LAN; wss:// when TLS is
# terminated at a reverse proxy in front of the hub.
#   ws://<hub-ip>:8765/agent/connect
#   wss://hub.example.com/agent/connect
hub_url=ws://<hub-ip>:8765/agent/connect

# Must match ALB_AGENT_TOKEN on the hub. Stick to letters/digits/_/- so the
# same value pastes cleanly into the hub's hub.env too.
# Generate one:  openssl rand -hex 16
token=change-me

# Label shown in the hub's Connection Center.
name=bench-01

# Stable agent identity. Generate once and keep it — without it the hub sees
# a brand-new agent after every restart. Any unique string works.
#agent_id=

# Local status page (http://127.0.0.1:<port>). 0 disables. Default 8731.
#status_port=8731

# Status page bind host. Keep the default 127.0.0.1 — anything else exposes
# the page beyond this machine.
#status_host=127.0.0.1

# Run log: every run appends here (rotates at ~5 MB, keeps 3 old files) so
# problems can be analyzed after the console window is closed. Relative
# paths are resolved next to this script. Set to none to disable.
log_file=logs/agent.log

# ── fastboot / flashing (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Path to fastboot on THIS machine. Leave unset to use whatever is on PATH;
# leave it unresolvable and this agent simply does not advertise the
# `fastboot` capability — the hub then answers "this bench cannot flash"
# immediately instead of letting a caller discover it by timing out.
#   fastboot_path=D:\platform-tools\fastboot.exe
#fastboot_path=

# Partition allowlist for `alb flash write`. Empty = any well-formed name is
# accepted (names are still shape-checked here: no separators, no spaces, no
# leading dash, since the name becomes an argv element). Set it when a bench
# should only ever write a known set — flashing the wrong partition is not
# undoable and the board may not come back to report it.
#   flash_partitions=vendor_cfg,boot,dtbo
#flash_partitions=

# One bench host, several boards swapped on its cable: give each board its
# own list instead (ADR-063). The board names are the hub's board table
# names (`alb bench board`); the hub sends the board it has chosen with
# every job. With any per-board list set:
#   - a partition op must name a board that has a list here, and the
#     partition must be on THAT board's list — otherwise FLASH_BOARD_NOT_LISTED
#     or FLASH_PARTITION_REJECTED, before anything is written;
#   - flash_partitions above must stay unset (the agent refuses to start);
#   - an empty per-board line is refused too: dropped, it would widen the
#     list to any name (or skip the product check).
# fastboot_product.<board> is what `fastboot getvar product` answers on that
# board. Set it and every write first asks the device; another board on the
# cable is refused with FLASH_BOARD_MISMATCH and nothing is written.
#   flash_partitions.board-a=boot,dtbo,vendor_cfg
#   fastboot_product.board-a=board_a_product
#   flash_partitions.board-b=boot_a,boot_b,dtbo_a,dtbo_b

# ── Qualcomm 9008 / EDL flashing (optional, ADR-066) ──────────────────
# For boards that only take an image in Qualcomm's emergency download mode
# (USB 05c6:9008, "Qualcomm HS-USB QDLoader 9008 (COMnn)"). Set all three
# and the agent offers edl-sahara / edl-program / edl-reset; set some and it
# logs which one is missing and offers none.
#   edl_sahara   — QSaharaServer.exe (loads the firehose programmer)
#   edl_firehose — fh_loader.exe     (writes what the XML lists, resets)
#   edl_root     — the directory the unpacked factory packages sit under.
#                  The hub names a package by its directory RELATIVE to this
#                  root (`dir=<package>\<subdir>`); anything that resolves
#                  outside it is refused. Any package under it can be used.
# The 9008 COM port is found from the USB device list; never set here.
# Suggested layout: one directory per board, named as in the hub's board
# table, holding everything that board's flashing needs. The root is not
# named after one vendor's mode: other vendors' tools go under it too.
#   D:\flash\board-a\tools\       vendor tools, plus a note of where they came from
#   D:\flash\board-a\packages\    unpacked packages, one directory each, original names
#   D:\flash\board-a\drivers\     driver installers, for a reinstall
#   D:\flash\board-a\README.txt    how to enter the mode, which XML to use
# The three keys are one set for the whole host and cannot be set per board:
# with one kind of 9008 board on the host, point them at that board's directory.
#   edl_sahara=D:\flash\board-a\tools\QSaharaServer.exe
#   edl_firehose=D:\flash\board-a\tools\fh_loader.exe
#   edl_root=D:\flash\board-a\packages
#edl_sahara=
#edl_firehose=
#edl_root=

# ── Other vendors' download-mode tools (optional, ADR-068) ────────────
# Register a vendor's command-line flashing tool under a name of your choice
# and the hub can run it with the tool's OWN arguments:
#   alb flash tool <name> --pkg <package dir> -- <the tool's arguments>
# Only tools registered here can run; the hub never names a program or a
# path. Before running, the agent refuses any argument holding a drive path
# (X:\ or X:/), a network path (\\server, //server), a path from the drive
# root (\x) or '..' — name files relative to the package (the tool runs in
# it) or write {pkg}. {com} becomes the COM port of the tool_usb device.
# `alb flash tools` lists what is registered, with the usage text below.
#
# Name rule: 1-32 of a-z 0-9 _ -, starting with a letter or digit.
#   tool_exe.<name>     REQUIRED. Absolute path of the tool's .exe.
#   tool_root.<name>    REQUIRED. Packages sit in directories under it; the
#                       hub names one by its path relative to this root.
#   tool_cwd.<name>     package (default): run in the package directory, as
#                       you would by hand; job: in a fresh scratch directory.
#   tool_timeout.<name> seconds, 1-1700, default 1500. The tool and every
#                       process it started are ended after this.
#   tool_ok.<name>      regex; a line must match it for the run to count as a
#                       success. Without it only the exit code is looked at,
#                       and the result says so — some tools exit 0 on failure.
#   tool_fail.<name>    regex; a matching line makes the run a failure, even
#                       with exit code 0.
#   tool_note.<name>    regex; matching lines are listed in the result
#                       (e.g. one line per partition written).
#   tool_usb.<name>     VID:PID, comma-separated. Checked before running: the
#                       board must be attached in that mode. Needed for {com}.
#   tool_usage.<name>   notes shown by `alb flash tools`: how to enter the mode,
#                       which arguments to give. Repeat the line for more lines.
# Files the tool writes into its working directory (its own logs) come back
# to the hub next to the job's record. A missing tool_exe or tool_root file
# is logged at start-up and that tool is not offered; a malformed line stops
# the agent from starting.
#
# Example — fastboot registered as a pass-through tool, to compare with the
# built-in fastboot ops:
#   tool_exe.fb=D:\platform-tools\fastboot.exe
#   tool_root.fb=D:\flash\board-a\packages
#   tool_fail.fb=FAILED
#   tool_usage.fb=board in fastboot first: alb power reboot fastboot
#   tool_usage.fb=e.g. alb flash tool fb --pkg v12 -- flash boot boot.img
